// bisect: fusion/JIT bug (interp != jit) — FIXED, kept as regress pin.
// The TYPECHECKER was the wrong one; after the fix this program is a
// CompileErr in both modes (AGREE), like arith-tc-unsound-jul2026.
// Found by soak aug14e (katana fuzz divergence_000001, 2026-08-14);
// reduced from a struct + slice pattern to the essential shape.
//
// Pre-fix: interp i64:0, jit i64:1, with the kernel logging
//   kernel param `v`: runtime F64(1.0) doesn't match the compiled
//   Scalar(I64) slot (typechecker static/dynamic mismatch)
//
// A select arm's GUARD was typechecked in `Select::typecheck0`'s FIRST
// loop, before the second loop's
//     ntype.contains(&ctx.env, &pat.type_predicate.any_as_tvar())
// narrows the arm's pattern binds to the scrutinee type. So the guard
// saw `v` as a still-open TVar and the first use inside it BOUND the
// cell: `p: fn(i64) -> bool` bound `v := i64` even though the scrutinee
// is `[i64, f64]`. The identical call in the arm BODY is correctly
// rejected — bodies typecheck after the narrowing — which is what made
// the asymmetry findable:
//     select u { v => p(v) }         rejected   (body, narrowed)
//     select u { v if p(v) => .. }   ACCEPTED   (guard, not yet narrowed)
//
// The node-walk then compared dynamically (Value::partial_cmp is total
// across types, so f64:1.0 > i64:0 is true) while the kernel froze the
// param to Scalar(I64) per the now-wrong static type and bottomed the
// f64 at the ABI boundary — the guard read false and the `_` arm won.
//
// FIX: move the guard's own `typecheck0` out of the first loop and into
// the second, immediately before the bool check that was moved there
// earlier for the SAME reason (its comment: "so that a guard which IS
// the arm's own bind reports against the bind's settled type"). The
// narrowing is per-arm and progressive — `ntype` is diffed by each
// earlier unguarded irrefutable arm — so the second loop is the only
// place the settled type exists.
//
// Note the guard must move, not just be re-run: the premature bind is
// destructive (the cell is bound for the rest of compilation), so
// re-checking later would compare against the type the guard itself
// chose.
{let p = |x| x > i64:0; select {let x = [f64:1., f64:2.]; select x {[x, y, ..] => x, _ => i64:0}} {v if p(v) => i64:0, _ => i64:1}}
